EXECUTIVE DELTA

The highest-information update is a correction to the Street Manager
access model. There are two different near-real-time mechanisms that
should not be conflated. Street Manager's authenticated API
Notifications endpoint subscribes an authenticated organisation and is
organisation-scoped. Separately, DfT's public roadworks-data guidance
says England-wide roadworks data is publicly viewable free of charge
via API after registration, and the public feed exposes
event_reference, work_reference_number, permit_reference_number,
coordinates, work category, traffic-management type, proposed/actual
dates, work status, USRN, event_time, object_reference and version.
Therefore the Street Manager =C3=97 BODS OutcomeGraph remains plantable,
but POW should use the public/open-data registration path for national
observation and reserve authenticated API Notifications for a
participating authority/promoter/contractor integration.

This run also sharpens the reconstruction test. National raw BODS is
definitively not a moat: National Data Library archives SIRI-VM every
~30 seconds, currently 2.74 TB, under OGL, with known collection gaps;
its main page says the archive began 18 June 2025 and identifies a
University of Exeter archive back to February 2024. The moat is the
continuously resolved intervention =E2=86=92 exposure =E2=86=92 degradation=
 =E2=86=92 recovery
graph, plus gap filling where public archives fail.

A second important identity lesson comes from EPC. Current official
statistics explicitly note multiple EPCs can exist for the same
dwelling and that de-duplication uses UPRN plus address; address-level
open data is updated daily through the developer API. A government
linkage example achieved a 97% EPC match among social lettings with
valid UPRNs. UPRN should therefore be POW's primary site identity
whenever present, with address matching as a resolver rather than a
canonical key.

